# 🖥️ Kali Linux Pen-Testing Lab on VirtualBox


![Kali Linux](https://img.shields.io/badge/Kali-Linux%202026.2-blue)


![VirtualBox](https://img.shields.io/badge/Hypervisor-VirtualBox%207.2.4-lightgrey)


![Purpose](https://img.shields.io/badge/Purpose-Ethical%20Hacking-green)


## 📌 Overview
This project documents how I built an isolated virtual lab for cybersecurity practice using VirtualBox and Kali Linux on Windows 11. The lab runs on a private NAT Network, so I can learn and test tools safely without risking my main computer or home network.

## 🎯 Objectives
- Install and configure VirtualBox
- Install/import Kali Linux as a virtual machine
- Create a private NAT Network for the lab
- Configure network connectivity for Kali Linux
- Assign a consistent IP address to the Kali VM
- Verify network connectivity and DNS resolution
- Take a clean VM snapshot for recovery
- Document the complete setup process
- Prepare the environment for future cybersecurity projects

## 🏗️ Lab Architecture
```
Windows 11 Host
   └── VirtualBox 7.2.4
         └── NAT Network "NatNetwork" (10.0.0.0/24)
               ├── Kali Linux VM (10.0.0.10)
               └── Future target VMs
```

## ⚙️ Lab Configuration
| Component | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Host OS | Windows 11 |
| Host RAM | 8 GB |
| Hypervisor | VirtualBox 7.2.4 |
| Security OS | Kali Linux 2026.2 (pre-built VirtualBox image, amd64) |
| Kali VM RAM / CPUs | ___ MB / ___ |
| Virtual Network | NAT Network (NatNetwork) |
| Network Address | 10.0.0.0/24 |
| Kali IP Address | 10.0.0.10/24 |
| Default Gateway | 10.0.0.1 |
| DNS Server | 8.8.8.8 |

## 🪜 Setup Procedure

### Step 1: Install 7-Zip
Kali's pre-built VM is distributed as a `.7z` archive, so I installed 7-Zip to extract it.

### Step 2: Install VirtualBox
Downloaded VirtualBox 7.2.4 for Windows from virtualbox.org. Windows asked for permission, and the publisher was verified as Oracle America, Inc.



![VirtualBox Manager](images/01-virtualbox-manager.png)



### Step 3: Download and Import Kali Linux
Downloaded the pre-built VirtualBox image (`kali-linux-2026.2-virtualbox-amd64`) from kali.org/get-kali, extracted it with 7-Zip, and imported it into VirtualBox.



![Kali VM](images/02-kali-vm.png)



### Step 4: Create the NAT Network
In VirtualBox: Tools > Network > NAT Networks > Create.
- Network name: NatNetwork
- IPv4 prefix: 10.0.0.0/24
- DHCP: Enabled

I chose a NAT Network because VMs on it can talk to each other and still reach the internet, which suits a multi-machine lab.



![NAT Network](images/03-nat-network.png)



### Step 5: Attach Kali to the NAT Network
Kali's Adapter 1 was originally set to the default NAT (IP 10.0.2.15, gateway 10.0.2.2). I changed it to **NAT Network** and selected NatNetwork.



![Adapter settings](images/04-adapter.png)



### Step 6: Assign a Static IP
Found my connection name with `nmcli connection show`, then set a fixed IP:
```
sudo nmcli connection modify "Wired connection 1" ipv4.method manual ipv4.addresses 10.0.0.10/24 ipv4.gateway 10.0.0.1 ipv4.dns 8.8.8.8
sudo nmcli connection up "Wired connection 1"
```
A consistent IP makes the lab easier to document and reference.



![Static IP](images/05-static-ip.png)



### Step 7: Take a Clean Snapshot
Created a snapshot named `Kali-Clean-Baseline` as a recovery point before any testing.



![Snapshot](images/06-snapshot.png)



## 🔎 Verification
| Test | Command | Result |
| Check IP | `ip a` | ✅ |
| Ping gateway | `ping -c 4 10.0.0.1` | ✅ |
| Test internet | `ping -c 4 8.8.8.8` | ✅ |
| Test DNS | `ping -c 4 google.com` | ✅ |
| Check Nmap | `nmap --version` | ✅ |


![Verification](images/07-verification.png)


## 💡 What I Learned
- A standard NAT isolates each VM, while a NAT Network lets VMs talk to each other and still reach the internet.
- A static IP and a clean snapshot make a lab predictable and easy to recover.
- Documenting every step makes the lab easy to reproduce.
- 

## 🔐 Ethical Use
This lab is for educational purposes only. Only test systems you own or have written permission to test.

## 🚀 Next Steps
- Add a vulnerable target (Metasploitable or DVWA) to the same network
- Run my first Nmap scans
- Practice vulnerability scanning and web testing

